    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "gremlins?? lol that's a funny way to put it. i guess i can see why people call them that if they're just constantly popping up to mess things up. \n\nbut seriously, how do they actually \"steal\" the energy? like, if it's just a parasitic capacitance, is it actually absorbing some of the power, or is it just delaying the signal or something? it feels like they're almost like little thieves hiding in the traces.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A parasitic capacitance, or inductance, doesn't necessarily \"steal\" power in the sense of a direct drain, but rather it *redirects* the signal's energy in ways that are detrimental to the intended circuit function. It's less like a thief taking loot and more like a mischievous prankster diverting the path of the signal, causing it to take a longer, slower route or oscillate unexpectedly. Think of it like a tiny, invisible detour that forces your signal to spend time arguing with the surrounding environment instead of doing its job. These parasitic elements are the spectral manifestation of the tiny, un-idealized connections between every single trace and ground plane.",
      "source": "target",
